l_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-126547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,i+({x}^{2}-y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-126547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-126547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4dec808fbb78198391326e2601a173f95510b8bd3afbddce4d593ffa6479681a"} {"question" "q-126547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-126547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)"] ["math" "2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-126547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/4c38294780ac6d5079b759f39a7dbd4944b1c3c21495245dfd314ec180c0434e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4dec808fbb78198391326e2601a173f95510b8bd3afbddce4d593ffa6479681a"} {"question" "q-105671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105092" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105092" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105092" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4dec808fbb78198391326e2601a173f95510b8bd3afbddce4d593ffa6479681a"} {"question" "q-105092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/2414de6e2129d4fceaae304d16f9be0c91a545e5a8544b2033f353ddb2e581b9.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4dec808fbb78198391326e2601a173f95510b8bd3afbddce4d593ffa6479681a"} {"question" "q-105669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/773ff9d880deed92f8034d6949cfee85edbcdf735c8e125f1ec5762137fadb9c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/4e344a0a1f6b0c535c5ba08ee8c87914cf5e7009600f45732023720048b4c493.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statements IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4dec808fbb78198391326e2601a173f95510b8bd3afbddce4d593ffa6479681a"} {"question" "q-105667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II, III, and IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/d0a06b24d18fac0e76a6d6f7cfc5409288c1b4dc7b1325d45b0ef30a8e3362f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has a curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/6cb2333688e1a4a255398262d43046554c817705f09f7be9d6e6d082af519c42.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4dec808fbb78198391326e2601a173f95510b8bd3afbddce4d593ffa6479681a"} {"question" "q-105692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II and IV  only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "q-105692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and IV  only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/state.json" "file_sha256" "e834a2341c6e1f18464a75d5fa46289659b30f3fc44107666214c011a5735328"} {"question" "e-8743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $\\left(-1,0,2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/example-8743.json" "file_sha256" "11933e9d04040e3aa206ba945ce43ab49910d063b813292c16bb8f1b079f8edc"} {"question" "e-8743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/888b12c4f687dba1199a82d7e3a5e6897e7599ea812753423de5a23ffd756888.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & {x}^{2} & -2z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-0)j+(2x-1)k \\\\  & =(2x-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-1,0,2) & =(2(-1)-1)\\,k=-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/example-8743.json" "file_sha256" "11933e9d04040e3aa206ba945ce43ab49910d063b813292c16bb8f1b079f8edc"} {"question" "e-9556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/example-9556.json" "file_sha256" "2e1d443769e31ea8423d2ad5aa0dfa4f6957f5e6659db6c09213ab906bb8e543"} {"question" "e-9556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =3y-{e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =3(1)-{e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =3-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/example-9556.json" "file_sha256" "2e1d443769e31ea8423d2ad5aa0dfa4f6957f5e6659db6c09213ab906bb8e543"} {"question" "e-8929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/example-8929.json" "file_sha256" "9bb39630a2e484059842bceab8c97994e3cb4721afe0023bed5f86d03ff54634"} {"question" "e-8929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$ everywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/example-8929.json" "file_sha256" "9bb39630a2e484059842bceab8c97994e3cb4721afe0023bed5f86d03ff54634"}] "usage" {"e-8929" [] "e-8743" [] "e-9556" [] "q-105092" [] "q-105668" []} "basis" 1677 "retractions" [[17592186133984 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/q-105092.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z)$, shown above. Given that $F(x, y, z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $curlF = 0$\n2. $curlF \\ne 0$\n3. $F$ is irrotational\n4. $curlF$ points in the negative $z$-direction."] [17592186133616 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right)$."] [17592186133616 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncurlF &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & x^{2} & -2z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2})]i - [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j + [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\\n&= (0 - 0)i - (0 - 0)j + (2x - 1)k \\\\\n&= (2x - 1)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,0,2\\right)\n$$\n\nand obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(curlF)(-1, 0, 2) &= (2(-1) - 1)k =-3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133637 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}$. Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1)$."] [17592186133637 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{x + y}) \\\\\n&= 3y - e^{x + y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})_{(-1, 1)} &= 3(1) - e^{-1 + 1} \\\\\n&= 3 - e^{0} \\\\\n&= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133620 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$, shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/a7e6add34274406286967ea9c1d41886.png)\n\n1. $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n2. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n3. $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n4. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction."] [17592186133620 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/83eaadb4dc59f6f9352c6d5c3d9901b6.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186130468 17592186130469 17592186130470 17592186130471 17592186130472 17592186294436 17592186294437 17592186294438 17592186294439 17592186294440] "immutable_fields" [[17592186130467 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130467 :answer-field/id] [17592186130467 :answer-field/key] [17592186130467 :answer-field/type] [17592186294435 :answer-field/correct] [17592186294435 :answer-field/id] [17592186294435 :answer-field/key] [17592186294435 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
